  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Unit: YM-000026, issue #26; claim and source pin are unchanged.

    <item>Status: draft audit; mathematical and Lean-correspondence review
    pending.

    <item>Scope: immediate chain from the flat entrance estimate to the
    quantitative cutoff comparison, plus the first irreducible per-step RG
    input.

    <item>Outputs for this bounded follow-up: this file only. The finalized
    lattice manuscript is unchanged.

    <item>Source revision: <verbatim|3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464>.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Conclusion><label|conclusion>

  The rate is not obtained by merely summing a geometric tail of an already
  given kernel estimate. The source first proves a one-step propagation
  estimate for a neutral curvature representative of the difference of two
  quadratic RG precisions. This estimate yields a recurrence for a positive
  weighted coefficient cost. A separate, elementary exponential-tilt argument
  turns that recurrence into a power of the RG scale ratio. A Cauchy argument
  then constructs the limiting kernel.

  The scale-recursion and limit arguments can be explained from explicit
  scalar inequalities. The first genuinely RG-specific step is the private
  <verbatim|to_here> helper in <verbatim|Lean/YM/RG08/Flat/World.lean>, which
  applies the fully qualified theorem <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.toward> in
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/RG08/Packets/FlatStep.lean>. Its hypotheses include exact
  stationarity and quadratic-floor data, atlas bounds on input kernels, an
  exact tower identity, and neutrality/travel conditions. Its conclusion
  propagates a curvature representative and its folded cost through a
  Schur-complement step. I did not prove that mathematical lemma
  independently. It is the smallest next source declaration to assign for a
  detailed mathematical proof. A separate scalar proposition is supplied in
  <hlink|<verbatim|manuscript/existence/flat-estimate.tex>|https://github.com/hairer/humanise-yang-mills/blob/main/manuscript/existence/flat-estimate.tex>,
  labels <verbatim|prop:ex-lattice-flat-rate> and
  <verbatim|rem:ex-lattice-flat-rate-scope>. It proves only the
  recurrence-to-power-rate implication. The full flat-sector estimate remains
  open until the untranslated RG step is proved and its hypotheses are
  verified on the selected construction data.

  <section|Exact local chain><label|exact-local-chain>

  All declarations below are from the pinned source revision. Paths are
  relative to <verbatim|Lean/>.

  <subsection|1. Entrance reshapes one schedule, then returns to the original
  RG data><label|1-entrance-reshapes-one-schedule-then-returns-to-the-original-rg-data>

  <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.entrance_gate> (<verbatim|YM/RG08/Flat/Entrance.lean>)
  starts with a simple gauge group, kernel choices, a supply sheet, and
  equality of its kernel with the selected kernel. It obtains a retained
  linear plan, changes the sheet's stage clips by positive factors, and
  applies that plan. For an arbitrary compatible consumer level sequence and
  length floor, it then sets <verbatim|b := shadow a \<gamma\> HC.fit> and
  invokes <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.spacing_choice> on <verbatim|b>. From the
  returned separation schedule and a sufficient horizon, it invokes
  <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.supplies> and transports the result back to the
  original horizon using <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.same_shadow>.

  The shadow is defined in <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.shadow>
  (<verbatim|YM/RG08/Flat/Shadow.lean>). It changes the <verbatim|Stage.clip>
  factorization while preserving the schedule, RG levels, block lengths,
  couplings, and quadratic precision data.
  <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.same_shadow> proves equality of the evaluated flat
  kernels, conditional on sufficient-horizon hypotheses on both schedules. It
  does not identify other higher-order or auxiliary data built from the two
  histories.

  <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.spacing_choice> (<verbatim|YM/RG08/Flat/Length.lean>)
  supplies a <verbatim|Spacing> record with width
  <verbatim|min(1,min(P.\<alpha\> j,P.\<alpha\>(j+1)))>, exponent
  <verbatim|\<theta\>=1/2>, and age function <verbatim|1+Lambda(a)> (where
  <verbatim|Lambda=2+log(hatBeta)>). Its numerical inputs are:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>length floors at least the ceiling of the explicit entrance cost
    <verbatim|2/curve + before(j,j)^2 + before(j,j-1)^2>;

    <item>a delay bound <verbatim|P.\<Omega\>(J_i) \<leq\> log(hatBeta_i)>
    for all <verbatim|i> after the supplied threshold;

    <item>simplicity, used for the positive RG slope that bounds the RG age
    by the logarithmic coupling.
  </itemize-minus>

  The source proof of this helper is elementary: the fit condition makes
  adjacent <verbatim|J_i> indices either equal or consecutive; the chosen
  entrance cost pays both the contraction quote and width-crossing
  inequalities, while the delay and coupling slope give the polynomial age
  cap. It is a finite numerical estimate, not the cutoff comparison itself.

  <subsection|2. RowCost is an absolute, exponentially weighted summability
  bound><label|2-rowcost-is-an-absolute-exponentially-weighted-summability-bound>

  <verbatim|YM.Pert.Bulk.RowCost> is defined in
  <verbatim|YM/Pert/Bulk/Atlas.lean>. For a kernel <verbatim|K> between
  cover-markets, a side length <verbatim|L>, width <verbatim|\<sigma\>>, and
  cost <verbatim|p>, its meaning is

  <\equation*>
    \<forall\>u,j,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|y\<in\><math-up|Cover>><math-up|ofReal><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<around|\||K<around|(|u,<around|(|y,j|)>|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|\<sigma\>*<space|0.17em>\<delta\><rsub|L><around|(|u<rsub|<math-up|pos>>,y|)>>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|ofReal><around|(|p|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <verbatim|\<delta\>_L(x,y)=sep(x,L.val\\,y)/L.val>, where
  <verbatim|sep> is the Laurent-size distance on the full cover. It is a
  rescaled cover distance, not finite-torus distance. Thus it controls
  absolute summability of every row, with an exponential spatial weight; it
  is stronger than pointwise kernel convergence. The associated
  <verbatim|Ticket> bound has the same weighted absolute-sum form.
  <verbatim|Flat.output> in <verbatim|YM/RG08.Flat.Curl> contracts a packet
  with two fixed coboundary kernels; <verbatim|Flat.sigil_row> turns a
  <verbatim|Folded> packet bound into <verbatim|RowCost> of this output.

  <subsection|3. One RG step propagates the curvature
  representative><label|3-one-rg-step-propagates-the-curvature-representative>

  <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.FlatRep> in <verbatim|YM/RG08/Flat/World.lean>
  contains four conditions on a packet representing the cutoff difference at
  a level: <verbatim|Folded> weighted absolute summability over all packet
  coordinates, neutrality (the packet coefficient sum vanishes),
  <verbatim|town 1> invariance under the simultaneous unit shift, and a
  <verbatim|Sigil> identity saying its exterior-derivative contraction agrees
  with the difference of the two quadratic precisions on every torus in the
  period family.

  The private <verbatim|to_here> helper transports a <verbatim|FlatRep> from
  level <verbatim|i+1> to level <verbatim|i>. Its conclusion is a pushed
  packet, with new folded cost multiplied by the source coefficient
  <verbatim|quote(a,P,i,u)>. Its proof invokes <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.toward>
  with, among other data:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>the two histories' exact <verbatim|Diagonal> identities and
    <verbatim|Floor> bounds;

    <item>order-two <verbatim|Atlas> bounds and <verbatim|city> conditions
    for the two curvature kernels;

    <item>exact matching of each history's one-step tower with the
    destination quadratic update;

    <item>neutrality, <verbatim|town 1>, folded-cost, and travel properties
    of the previous packet; and

    <item>the previous level's <verbatim|Sigil> identity.
  </itemize-minus>

  The output of <verbatim|toward> preserves neutrality and <verbatim|town 1>,
  multiplies the folded cost by the exact coefficient
  <verbatim|neutralCoin(L, Faces, Faces, \<tau\>, q, p) = \|Faces\| q
  (\|Faces\|(2/\<tau\>)p)/L>, and proves the next <verbatim|Sigil> identity.
  The <verbatim|Sigil> step uses a mixed Schur identity
  (<verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.poised>) and the exact <verbatim|Diagonal>
  hypotheses. The proof of the folded bound uses the source-specific
  <verbatim|push_null> estimate. These are substantive RG statements; they
  cannot be replaced by the numerical spacing estimate.

  <subsection|4. World.overlap solves a discrete weighted
  recurrence><label|4-worldoverlap-solves-a-discrete-weighted-recurrence>

  The private helper <verbatim|existsRows> in
  <verbatim|YM/RG08/Flat/World.lean> starts from an endpoint packet
  constructed by <verbatim|Flat.at_end>. That endpoint packet is the
  difference between the two saddle kernels at the top cutoff. Its cost is
  the top allowance. Working downward one stage at a time, it applies
  <verbatim|to_here>. The explicit scalar cost majorant is

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|i>=C<rsub|<math-up|top>>*<space|0.17em>W<rsub|i><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>W<rsub|i>=exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|(|\<theta\>*<around|(|t<rsub|<math-up|top>>-t<rsub|i>|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <verbatim|t_i> is cumulative logarithmic RG scale. Since
  <verbatim|W_i^{-1}=W_{i+1}^{-1}\\exp(-\\theta(t_{i+1}-t_i))>, the
  propagated packet cost is bounded by the next-stage cost times
  <verbatim|drop_i = exp(-\<theta\>(t_{i+1}-t_i))>, plus a top-only source.
  The theorem <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.World.overlap> combines this recurrence
  with the paid-quote, block-width, and top-cost receipts in its
  <verbatim|Funding> hypothesis. It applies
  <verbatim|YM.RG08.Rate.Stair.fade_solve> to obtain constants
  <verbatim|A\<geq\>0>, integer <verbatim|d>, and <verbatim|c\<gtr\>0> such
  that the folded cost at level <verbatim|i> is at most

  <\equation*>
    A*<space|0.17em><around*|(|2+Z+<around|\||t<rsub|i>-t<rsub|k>|\|>|)><rsup|d><around*|(|<frac|a<rsub|<math-up|top>>|a<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|c>.
  </equation*>

  This is the geometric/decay part of the argument, but only after the
  one-step packet propagation and all funding receipts have been established.

  <subsection|5. The recurrence-to-rate lemma is separately
  proved><label|5-the-recurrence-to-rate-lemma-is-separately-proved>

  The source uses <verbatim|YM.RG08.Rate.Stair.fade_solve>
  (<verbatim|YM/RG08/Rate/Ascent.lean>). The new manuscript proposition is a
  finite-interval variant, proved in ordinary mathematical language; it is
  not a translation of the source theorem statement or a proof of the RG
  estimate that supplies the recurrence.

  For the source application, set the finite interval endpoint to the top RG
  level, take <verbatim|s> to be the reporting scale <verbatim|t_k>, and let
  <verbatim|Z> be the nonnegative schedule offset. The source age is exactly
  <verbatim|2+Z+\|t_i-t_k\|>. The scalar proposition uses
  <verbatim|R+Z+\|t_i-t_k\|> with fixed <verbatim|R=max(2,2(d+1)/(theta-mu))>,
  so its constants do not depend on the reporting scale or the schedule
  offset. The source's polynomial bound in its smaller age therefore implies
  the proposition's bound in the larger age, provided the forcing and
  endpoint coefficient are uniform across schedules. The <verbatim|Spacing>
  age-cap and <verbatim|Funding.top_cap> fields are the source receipts used
  to obtain that uniformity in <verbatim|World.overlap>. Matching the
  source's exact recurrence, forcing and endpoint to the proposition remains
  a correspondence step; it does not discharge the per-step packet estimate.

  The scalar proof tilts by <verbatim|exp(mu*(T-t_i))>, with
  <verbatim|mu=min(c_0,theta/2)>. The resulting contraction is
  <verbatim|exp(-(theta-mu)*Delta_i)>. Polynomial age growth is absorbed by
  choosing an additive age offset at least <verbatim|2(d+1)/(theta-mu)>,
  leaving a uniform geometric factor <verbatim|exp(-(theta-mu)*log(3)/2)\<less\>1>.
  Backward induction sums the forcing with constant <verbatim|A/(1-q)>.
  Undoing the tilt yields the factor <verbatim|exp(-mu*(T-t_i))=(a_top/a_i)^mu>.
  The full proof, including the finite terminal case and zero-cost case, is
  in the manuscript proposition.

  <subsection|6. Kernel limit and conversion to the stated
  estimate><label|6-kernel-limit-and-conversion-to-the-stated-estimate>

  <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.Mesh.meshed> (<verbatim|YM/RG08/Flat/Mesh.lean>)
  instantiates <verbatim|World.overlap> on a road indexed by reporting scale,
  lower level, and finer cutoff. In addition to a sufficient horizon and
  <verbatim|Spacing>, its hypothesis requires exact equality between each
  actual tower's transfer matrix and the designated destination update. The
  theorem turns each <verbatim|FlatRep> into a <verbatim|Sigil> for the
  difference of the two cutoffs' quadratic precisions, applies
  <verbatim|Flat.resolve> to identify the output-kernel difference, and then
  applies <verbatim|Flat.sigil_row>. This yields the weighted RowCost Cauchy
  bound for two cutoffs.

  <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.Mesh.supplies> invokes
  <verbatim|YM.RG08.Packets.solvent> (<verbatim|YM/RG08/Packets/Limit.lean>).
  That completeness lemma has explicit hypotheses: a fixed reporting level,
  nonnegative spatial expense, a cutoff difference bound tending to zero,
  nonnegative error bills, and an eventual absolute-cost bound for one
  cutoff. It proves pointwise convergence to a kernel, retains the error
  ticket relative to the limit, and passes the cutoff cost bound to the
  limit. The proof is the Cauchy criterion in the complete target followed by
  Fatou/finite-sum lower semicontinuity for the weighted absolute costs. This
  part is not an additional RG estimate.

  <verbatim|Mesh.supplies> gives a separate limit kernel for each reporting
  clock label <verbatim|k>; uniqueness of pointwise limits proves they agree.
  It then multiplies the generic kernel cost by the fixed convolution
  constant from <verbatim|Flat.sigil_row>. This yields the public
  <verbatim|Flat.Quorum> fields: frame bound, cutoff convergence, and a
  cutoff-to-limit RowCost power estimate. The RowCost power estimate is not
  itself a theorem about the public Wilson measure or its smeared moments.

  <section|Smallest next mathematical task><label|smallest-next-mathematical-task>

  The next task should prove or independently review
  <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.toward> (<verbatim|Lean/YM/RG08/Packets/FlatStep.lean>)
  as one coherent one-step lemma. Required work:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>Read and state the meanings of <verbatim|Diagonal>,
    <verbatim|Floor>, <verbatim|Atlas>, <verbatim|city>, <verbatim|Matches>,
    <verbatim|neutral>, <verbatim|town>, <verbatim|Folded>,
    <verbatim|travels>, <verbatim|push_null>, and <verbatim|town_hall> used
    by its hypotheses and proof.

    <item>Derive the propagated <verbatim|Sigil> equality from the exact
    mixed Schur identity <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.poised> and the inherited
    precision identities.

    <item>Reproduce the <verbatim|push_null> weighted coefficient estimate
    from the stated atlas, floor, and geometry bounds, checking the constants
    and exponent in <verbatim|neutralCoin> and the <verbatim|Folded> bound.

    <item>Explain why the resulting neutral packet remains within the
    hypotheses of the next scale step.
  </enumerate>

  Do not expand the assignment to all higher-order flat kernels or the Wilson
  observable bridge: the local source explicitly limits this chain to the
  order-zero unmasked quadratic precision. Those additional components are
  separate requirements for applying the estimate to the full continuum
  theorem.

